34,2,,status,Midori,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
35,2,1,talk,May,,"So, I've got the data! It turns out one neighbor has been doing most of the complaining—which makes sense, since the bins are right at their gate. Also, our frontages are totally uneven. Some of us have plenty of room, others barely any.","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"@May, that explains the friction. However, we must be careful with the solution. I visited the station block's bin house and found that it traps smells in summer, making the air worse than an open pile.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Kenji,,"@May, @Mizuki, that's a lot to think about! I found out that we're spending 34,000 yen a year just on nets and smells. Plus, new shelters are really expensive—around 248,000 yen on average!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""May"",""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Midori,,"That's quite a lot of money, @Kenji. I'm also worried about the social side. I found that the station block started arguing over maintenance costs and cleaning duties shortly after building their shelter. We should be very careful.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Servas,,"My findings reveal a heavy administrative burden. A permanent structure requires a detailed site plan and four months of waiting. Furthermore, the truck arrives very early, meaning the labor begins before many have even woken.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,May,,"@Kenji, 248,000 yen is a huge jump! But @Servas, a four-month wait is just as bad. More importantly, if one person has been the main complainant, it's because they've been carrying the burden alone. We can't just buy a shelter and leave it there.","{""words"":44,""mentions"":[""Kenji"",""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@May, exactly. A shelter might seem like a fix, but as I saw at the station block, it can actually create a worse hygiene problem in August. We shouldn't trade a visual eyesore for a permanent smell.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Kenji,,"@May, @Mizuki, you're right! But wait, the ward gives a 40% subsidy up to 90,000 yen. Even then, it's still a lot of money. If we split the rest, will everyone be okay with that cost?","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""May"",""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@Kenji, even with a subsidy, that's a big expense. I'm more worried that a permanent structure might lead to the same arguments over cleaning and repairs that the station block had. We should choose something that doesn't create new tensions.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Servas,,"@Midori, your wisdom is sound. When we build walls, we often build barriers between people. If the labor begins before dawn and the paperwork is endless, we risk exhausting the very spirit of our neighborhood.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,May,,"@Servas, enough with the barriers! Let's be real: we have some neighbors with huge frontages and some with almost nothing. If we just ""split the cost"" but keep the bins in the same spot, it's not fair. We need a rotation or a move.","{""words"":44,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@May, a rotation sounds fair, but we must consider the daily reality. If we rotate the location, we might just move the smell and the nuisance from one person's gate to another's every few months.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Kenji,,"@May, @Mizuki, rotating sounds like a lot of work! But if we don't build a shelter, we still spend 34,000 yen a year on nets and deodorants. That's money we're losing every year just to keep things okay!","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""May"",""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Midori,,"@Kenji, I understand the waste, but I'm worried about the residents' meeting statements. People are reluctant to police each other's bags. If we choose a complex system, we might just create more reasons for neighbors to stop speaking.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Servas,,"@Kenji, the cost of nets is small compared to the cost of a weary soul. If we choose a path of complex rotations or rigid structures, we risk creating a chore that burdens the few. Let us seek simplicity.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,May,,"@Servas, simplicity is great, but not if it's simple for four of us and a nightmare for one. We have the measurements; we know who has the space. Let's make sure the final plan actually shares the physical burden.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Mizuki,,"@May, I agree that sharing the burden is key. However, we must avoid a ""solution"" that creates a hygiene nightmare. A shelter that traps smells or a rotation that just moves the nuisance isn't a real fix. We need a sustainable balance.","{""words"":42,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Kenji,,"@May, @Mizuki, I agree! But we can't forget the money. Whether we rotate or build, we need a plan that doesn't break anyone's budget. Let's find a way to be fair with both space and cash!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""May"",""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Midori,,"I hope we can find a middle ground. If we focus too much on who has the most space or who pays the most, we might forget to be kind to one another. Let's choose the most harmonious path.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Servas,,"Kindness is the foundation of any lasting agreement. Let us enter the final stage with open minds, remembering that the most elegant solution is one that asks the least of our time and our patience.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
